import asm65816
import AnimateEnemy_Main

/*
	batscr_codes:
	-------------
	This script is the main code file that holds all of the action/movement script-like codes
	and commands to use in your enemy scripts. If you are familiar with Catador's "movscr_codes", 
	you'll definitely find some similarity in how I structure and made each command, albiet worded
	slightly different, maybe even a bit esoteric. I also took inspiration from Cooprock's "Bowspr"
	script as well as I was developing the Bowspr Battle System, which also uses Action/Movement
	Scripts in battle.
	
	Special Thanks to PhoenixBound for helping me with the original Animate_Enemy_Main and the 
	other Animate_Enemy and Enemy_Float files. With his assistance on those files, it made me
	realize how stinky and confusing my original code was and how bad it was to even make new
	features for it. This script should make enemies do more things and stuff in battle
	much easier. There's still a ton of room to add or to create more features with, so if you 
	have an idea you can shoot me up a DM or just mention me in the PK Hack discord: D-Man
	
	* HOW TO USE *
	--------------
	If you are using the "AnimateEnemy_Main.ccs" file, you can assign an enemy to a "script" label. 
	Within that script label, you can use these commands to control how you want to display the
	enemy. For example, if you have an entry in the "AnimateEnemy_Main.ccs" file like so:
	
	animated_sprite(38, 8, SIZE_32x32, SIZE_128x64, Other_File.Label)
	
	Inside the "Other_File.ccs" file, your "Label" could look something like this:
	
	Label: {
		b_set_frame_random
		
		b_set_flying_height(10)
		b_set_flying_speed(5)
		b_rand_8bit
		b_set_flying_counter_result
		b_toggle_flying(1)
		
		b_set_var0(short[0] palette2)
		b_set_var1(short[1] palette2)
		b_toggle_palette_cycle(1)
		b_set_palette_cycle_rate(20)
	
	Loop:
		b_next_frame
	
		b_rand_8bit
		b_and_result(0x0003)
		b_set_palette_result
	
		b_rand_8bit
		b_and_result(0x0001)
		b_set_horizontal_result
	
		b_rand_8bit
		b_and_result(0x0001)
		b_set_vertical_result
	
		b_pause(30)
		b_jmp(Loop)
	}
	
	Don't worry about what any of these means just yet, just know you can go ham and crazy on
	what you can add do. 
	
	
	
	* EXAMPLES *
	------------
	1. 	I can take a guess that you're mostly here just to animate the enemies, so here's
		a simple script that will do the bare necessities. Assuming that you have an entry
		in the "AnimateEnemy_Main" file just like above, you can just animate them like so:
		
		Label: {
		Loop:
			b_next_frame
			b_pause(30)
			b_jmp(Loop)
		}
		
		This advances the enemy to their next frame and then does a 30 frame pause on the
		script. After that, it jumps back to the "Loop" label inside of the "Label" label
		to restart the whole script again. 
		
		IMPORTANT: 		ALWAYS INCORPORATE A "b_pause()" COMMAND SOMEWHERE WITHIN YOUR LOOP.
						Reason being is that you'll stall the game and softlock it because
						the script needs to give back control to the main game and it can only
						do that in a "b_pause()" command. 
		
	2.	If you have used my previous "Animate_Enemy_Main.ccs" file, you'll know that you
		have to give each entry a "speed" value. That speed value functions very similar
		to the "b_pause()" command here in this context. However, the speed value limits
		on the customizability of how you want to display each frame. Say you have a sprite
		that does a little shake and you want to emphasize it by having the sprite do a 
		fast update inbetween frames 2 and 5, with the old script you can't do that. But
		with this you can by doing something like this:
		
		Label: {
		Loop:
			b_set_frame(1)
			b_pause(30)
			b_set_frame(2)
			b_pause(2)
			b_set_frame(3)
			b_pause(3)
			b_set_frame(4)
			b_pause(2)
			b_set_frame(5)
			b_pause(5)
			b_set_frame(6)
			b_pause(20)
			b_set_frame(7)
			b_pause(25)
			b_set_frame(8)
			b_pause(15)
			
			b_jmp(Loop)
		}
		
	3. Given the previous example, you can display any frame of your sprite like so:
		
		Label: {												Label: {
		Loop:													Loop:
			b_set_frame(1)											// frame 1 is always displayed 1st
			b_pause(20)												b_pause(20)
			b_set_frame(2)		You can do it like this too			b_next_frame	// Display frame 2
			b_pause(10)					------->					b_pause(10)
			b_set_frame(3)											b_next_frame	// Display frame 3
			b_pause(5)												b_pause(5)
			b_set_frame(2)											b_prev_frame	// Display frame 2
			b_pause(10)												b_pause(10)
																	b_prev_frame	// Display frame 1
			b_jmp(Loop)												b_jmp(Loop)
		}														}
		
		You can go forwards or backwards or anywhere in between. If you intend to use
		"b_next_frame" and "b_prev_frame", you'll have to keep track of what frame your
		enemy is at, but it is more flexable than setting a specific frame. 
		
	4.	If you want your enemy to fly, you set a enemy's settings to make them fly like so:
		
		Label: {
			b_set_flying_speed(1)
			b_set_flying_height(10)
			b_toggle_flying(1)
			
		Loop:
			...
		}
		
		You'll always want set the enemy's flying settings at the start of the script first,
		then do the enemy's animations within the "Loop" label. 
		
		If you have used my previous "Animate_Enemy_Main" script, in order for an enemy to
		fly, you have to set an enemy's stat to some value. It works, however it wasn't as
		customizable per enemy usage as every enemy had the same height and speed value.
		You also weren't able to disable the flying if, say, an enemy was paralyzed or
		was feeling strange. This script should let you do that if you write your script
		to do that functionality, by default it doesn't so you'll have to make it up yourself.
		
		The value passed to "b_set_flying_speed" denotes when to update the y-position at each
		set frame. If you passed 1 to this command, it'll update the y-position at each frame.
		If you passed 2 to this command, it'll update the y-position at every 2 frames.
		
		The value passed to "b_set_flying_height" denotes how high your enemy can float. 
		The higher the number, the lower the height your enemy will float and slightly slows
		down the speed of which your enemy goes up and down. It's a weird inverse thing beucase
		this value actually is being used to divide the result of the sine function to limit
		how high or low the sprite should be, relative to the base y-position of the sprite.
		(NOTE TO DEV: IT BUGS WHEN YOU DISABLE FLYING WHEN YOU ENABLE IT IN THE FIRST PLACE
		SINCE THE Y-POSITION DOESN'T SNAP BACK TO THE BASE Y-POSITION WHEN DISABLED)
		
	5. If you want your enemies to cycle colors within their palette, you can do this:
	
		Label: {
			b_set_palette_cycle_rate(30)
			b_toggle_palette_cycle(1)
			
		Loop:
			...
		}
		
		This will make the palette cycle colors shifting every color to the left and
		wrapped around every 30 frames, one color at a time. You can control how fast
		or slow you want it to be by passing in a value to the "b_set_palette_cycle_rate"
		command.
		
		If, however, you want to keep some colors static and not cycle, you can use the
		"b_set_half_palette_cycle(1)" command at the beginning. This will only cycle colors
		from the first half of the palette, exclusing the transparency color (1st color).
		This is useful if you want to keep a core compontent of the sprite intact while
		having the rest of the colors be used for pulsating/shining/glowing or other uses
		just like how the battle backgrounds paltte cycle.
		
	6.	There's also another command that lets you palette cycle as well:
		
		Label: {
			b_set_result(0)
			
		Loop:
			b_add_result(1)
			b_jne_result(Loop_Back, 30)
			
			b_cycle_palette_left	// <--- this one here
			b_set_result(0)
			
		Loop_Back:
			b_pause(1)
			b_jmp(Loop)
		}
		
		This piece of script code functions behaviorly the same as the above script, but 
		this example here manually cycles the palette using a register value to keep track of 
		the frames and the "b_cycle_palette_left" command to cycle colors to the left once.
		There's also the "b_cycle_palette_right" command as well to cycle the palettes in the
		opposite direction. 
						
	As of right now, that's all of the major examples you'll need to start doing some cool stuff
	with your enemy scripts. You can scroll down to the COMMANDS section to see all of the avaiable
	commands you can use in your scripts. I'll make more examples as times goes on or when new
	features are implemented so you can get a look on how they are used. But for now, have fun
	with this and do some neat an cool things with your enemies.
	
	I'll also try to give each command their own descriptions soon, but for now I just wanna
	put this script out there before Jank Jam 2 starts so that people can explore and experiment
	with this first.
	
	WARNING:	DO NOT USE THE ENEMY EXTENDER BATTLE ACTION, IT WILL MESS UP THE IDs OF THE
				ENEMY AND THE CODE WILL TRY TO OVERWRITE TONS OF GARBAGE DATA IN RAM.
				YOU HAVE BEEN WARNED!!!!
				
*/

//+=+=+=+=+= COMMANDS =+=+=+=+=+
command b_halt {"[00]"}

command b_pause(value) 			{"[01 FF {byte value}]"}
command b_pause_result 			{"[01 00 00]"}
command b_pause_var0 			{"[01 01 00]"}
command b_pause_var1 			{"[01 02 00]"}
command b_pause_var2 			{"[01 03 00]"}
command b_pause_mem8(addr) 		{"[02 {short addr} 7E 00]"}
command b_pause_mem8_long(addr) {"[02 {long addr}]"}
command b_pause_mem8_var01		{"[03]"}

command b_set_result(value) 			{"[04 00 {short value}]"}
command b_set_result_mem8(addr) 		{"[05 00 00 {short addr} 7E 00]"}
